export const pkUnit09CommonInfoMarkdown: LocalizedText = {
  "en": "# PK Unit 9: Summer, Water and Outdoor Play — Common Info\n\n## 1. Unit Theme Overview\n\nPK Unit 9: Summer, Water and Outdoor Play is a summer extension unit for the non-language course block. It follows the same theme direction as PG Unit 9, but adjusts the expected participation level for PK children. The unit focuses on summer body care, water play routines, picnic participation, beach and garden materials, outdoor movement, shared helping, and safe warm-weather play.\n\nFor PK children, the eight non-language courses are still not language lessons. Each course type keeps its own developmental purpose: self-care, sensory exploration, story and puppet engagement, pretend role participation, creative expression, music and movement, construction and small-world play, and PSED and safety. The summer theme is used as a light story context, material source, routine reason, and English input pool.\n\nCompared with PG, PK children may use more short verbal responses, simple choices, role-play chunks, and small-group cooperation. They may attempt words such as `sun`, `water`, `hat`, `towel`, `wet`, `dry`, `picnic`, `bucket`, `leaf`, `stone`, `shell`, `hot`, `cool`, `safe body`, and short chunks such as `drink water`, `put it here`, `I need water`, `it is wet`, or `all done` when ready. However, independent verbal output and full sentence production are not required baseline outcomes.\n\n## 2. Unit Language\n\n### PK Core Vocabulary\n\n```text\nsun ｜ shade ｜ hot ｜ cool ｜ hat ｜ water ｜ water bottle ｜ towel ｜ wet ｜ dry ｜ cup ｜ fruit ｜ picnic ｜ basket ｜ napkin ｜ beach ｜ garden ｜ stone ｜ leaf ｜ bucket ｜ shell ｜ sand ｜ plant ｜ sponge ｜ mat ｜ rest ｜ safe body ｜ gentle hands ｜ helper\n```\n\n### Functional Teacher Chunks\n\n```text\nDrink water. ｜ Put on your hat. ｜ Stay in the shade. ｜ It is hot. ｜ It is cool. ｜ Wet hands. ｜ Dry hands. ｜ Wipe with the towel. ｜ Pour water. ｜ Scoop water. ｜ Squeeze the sponge. ｜ Put it in the bucket. ｜ Put it on the mat. ｜ Picnic time. ｜ Give me the cup. ｜ Put the fruit in the basket. ｜ Walk slowly. ｜ Stop. ｜ Wait. ｜ Gentle hands. ｜ Safe body. ｜ Clean up. ｜ All done.\n```\n\n## 3. Weekly Subthemes\n\n### Week 1: Summer Care — Sun, Shade, Hat and Water\n\nChildren practise summer body-care routines: drinking water, using hats, resting in shade, wiping, cooling down, and responding to `hot / cool` through real routines.\n\n### Week 2: Water Play — Wet, Dry, Pour, Scoop and Squeeze\n\nChildren explore water through safe sensory play with cups, sponges, towels, buckets, trays, and pouring/scooping routines.\n\n### Week 3: Picnic, Beach and Garden Materials\n\nChildren explore picnic baskets, fruit props, napkins, mats, stones, leaves, shells, sand props, buckets, and garden materials through sensory play, art, pretend play, and small-world construction.\n\n### Week 4: Outdoor Play, Helping and Safety\n\nChildren integrate summer care, water play, picnic participation, garden/beach materials, helper roles, clean-up, outdoor movement, shade/rest routines, and safe warm-weather play.\n\n## 4. Teacher Input Pool\n\n### Summer Care and Weather Input\n\nsun ｜ shade ｜ hot ｜ cool ｜ hat ｜ water ｜ water bottle ｜ towel ｜ rest ｜ mat ｜ drink water ｜ stay in the shade ｜ put on your hat ｜ wipe ｜ dry ｜ cool down\n\n### Water, Picnic, Beach and Garden Input\n\nwater ｜ wet ｜ dry ｜ cup ｜ sponge ｜ bucket ｜ pour ｜ scoop ｜ squeeze ｜ fruit ｜ picnic ｜ basket ｜ napkin ｜ beach ｜ garden ｜ stone ｜ leaf ｜ shell ｜ sand ｜ plant\n\n### Action and Response Input\n\nlook ｜ listen ｜ touch ｜ choose ｜ give ｜ put here ｜ put back ｜ drink water ｜ wipe ｜ pour ｜ scoop ｜ shake ｜ tap ｜ build ｜ walk slowly ｜ stop ｜ wait ｜ clean up ｜ gentle hands ｜ safe body ｜ helper\n\n### Teacher Routine Language\n\nDrink water. ｜ Hat on. ｜ Stay in the shade. ｜ It is hot. ｜ It is cool. ｜ Wet hands. ｜ Dry hands. ｜ Wipe with the towel. ｜ Pour water. ｜ Scoop water. ｜ Squeeze the sponge. ｜ Put it in the bucket. ｜ Picnic time. ｜ Walk slowly. ｜ Stop. ｜ Wait. ｜ Gentle hands. ｜ Safe body. ｜ Clean up. ｜ All done. ｜ Good job.\n\n## 5. Expected PK Response Modes\n\nChildren may respond by looking, pointing, choosing between two summer props, naming or attempting familiar words, putting on a hat with help, drinking water, wiping or drying hands, resting on a mat, pouring, scooping, squeezing sponges, placing objects in buckets or baskets, sorting wet/dry materials, setting up a picnic mat, giving cups or fruit props, building beach/garden scenes, walking slowly outdoors, stopping on cue, waiting for turns, helping clean up, and using short chunks such as `drink water`, `wet hands`, `dry hands`, `in the bucket`, `safe body`, or `all done` when ready.\n\nChildren are not expected to explain summer weather, describe beach or garden concepts, independently manage sun safety, produce full sentences, compare weather conditions, read labels, or complete worksheet-based summer tasks.\n\n## 6. Theme Songs / Chants\n\n### Core Songs\n\nRain Rain Go Away  \nI See Something Blue  \nWalking Walking  \nCount & Move  \nClean Up Song  \nPass the Beanbag  \nWalking In The Jungle  \nOpen Shut Them  \nThis Is The Way  \nThe Bath Song  \nAre You Hungry  \nThe More We Get Together\n\n### Use of Songs\n\nSongs should support water play, walking slowly, stopping, passing, picnic routines, wiping, washing, clean-up, resting, and shared helping. Children may sing repeated words when ready, but movement, gesture, object handling, pouring, scooping, shaking, tapping, wiping, and clean-up remain valid participation.\n\n## 7. Resource Opportunities\n\n### Useful EASTIE Spaces\n\nClassroom  \nSmall outdoor grass area attached to PK classroom  \nOutdoor grass area  \nSandpit  \nIndoor physical fitness area  \nScience classroom / water tray area  \nArt classroom  \nPretend kitchen / home corner  \nConstruction / block room  \nMusic classroom  \nCircle-time area / mat area  \nTable activity area\n\n### Useful Materials\n\nHats  \nTowels  \nWater bottles  \nCups  \nSponges  \nWater basins  \nWater trays  \nBuckets  \nScoops  \nFunnels  \nNapkins  \nPicnic baskets  \nFruit props  \nPicnic mats  \nLeaves  \nStones  \nShells  \nSand props  \nGarden tools  \nToy plants  \nBlocks  \nLego / Duplo bricks  \nLarge wooden blocks  \nCloths  \nShakers  \nRhythm sticks  \nBlue scarves  \nVisual hot/cool cards  \nGo/stop cards  \nClean-up baskets\n\n### Enhanced Resource Opportunities\n\nOutdoor grass and the attached classroom outdoor area can support shade/rest routines, water-bottle routines, picnic play, and slow movement games. The science classroom or water tray area can support pouring, scooping, squeezing, and wet/dry exploration. The sandpit and natural materials can support beach and garden small-world play. Art and construction spaces can support summer collages, beach/garden builds, and class festival displays if needed.\n\n## 8. Boundary Notes\n\nThis unit should not become a weather-science unit. `sun`, `hot`, `cool`, `shade`, `wet`, and `dry` are experienced through real routines and materials.\n\nThis unit should not require independent verbal output. Short chunks are readiness-based.\n\nThis unit should not use unsafe or unsupervised water play. Water depth, slipping, splashing, hygiene, allergies, and heat exposure must be controlled by adults.\n\nThis unit should not over-stage picnic or beach play. The developmental focus is concrete participation, helper roles, sensory exploration, safety, and clean-up.\n\n## 9. Unit-Level Observation Focus\n\nTeachers observe whether children participate in summer care routines, drink water, respond to shade/rest cues, use hats and towels with support, explore water safely, distinguish wet/dry through action, pour/scoop/squeeze with control, handle picnic/beach/garden materials, engage in pretend picnic or helper roles, build simple summer scenes, move safely outdoors, wait for turns, and join clean-up.\n\nObservation should focus on body-care awareness, sensory participation, self-regulation, safe movement, practical life skills, social helping, and readiness-based communication rather than vocabulary mastery.\n",
  "zh": "# PK Unit 9: Summer, Water and Outdoor Play — Common Info｜PK 单元 9：夏季、水与户外游戏 — 通用信息\n\n## 1. Unit Theme Overview｜单元主题概览\n\nPK 单元 9：夏季、水与户外游戏是面向非语言课程模块的夏季延伸单元。它沿用 PG 单元 9 的同一主题方向，但根据 PK 儿童的预期参与水平进行调整。本单元聚焦于夏季身体照护、玩水常规、野餐参与、海滩与花园材料、户外动作、共享帮忙和安全的热天游戏。\n\n对于 PK 儿童，八个非语言课程仍不是语言课。每种课程类型保持自己的发展目的：自我照护、感官探索、故事与木偶参与、假装角色参与、创意表达、音乐与动作、建构与小世界游戏，以及 PSED 与安全。夏季主题被用作轻量故事背景、材料来源、常规理由和英语输入库。\n\n和 PG 相比，PK 儿童可以使用更多简短的口头回应、简单的选择、角色游戏句块和小组合。他们可以尝试 `sun`、`water`、`hat`、`towel`、`wet`、`dry`、`picnic`、`bucket`、`leaf`、`stone`、`shell`、`hot`、`cool`、`safe body` 等单词，以及准备好时的简短句块，例如 `drink water`、`put it here`、`I need water`、`it is wet`、`all done`。然而，独立的口头输出和完整句子产出不是必需的基线结果。\n\n## 2. Unit Language｜单元语言\n\n### PK Core Vocabulary｜PK 核心词汇\n\nsun ｜ shade ｜ hot ｜ cool ｜ hat ｜ water ｜ water bottle ｜ towel ｜ wet ｜ dry ｜ cup ｜ fruit ｜ picnic ｜ basket ｜ napkin ｜ beach ｜ garden ｜ stone ｜ leaf ｜ bucket ｜ shell ｜ sand ｜ plant ｜ sponge ｜ mat ｜ rest ｜ safe body ｜ gentle hands ｜ helper\n\n### Functional Teacher Chunks｜实用教师句块\n\nDrink water. ｜ Put on your hat. ｜ Stay in the shade. ｜ It is hot. ｜ It is cool. ｜ Wet hands. ｜ Dry hands. ｜ Wipe with the towel. ｜ Pour water. ｜ Scoop water. ｜ Squeeze the sponge. ｜ Put it in the bucket. ｜ Put it on the mat. ｜ Picnic time. ｜ Give me the cup. ｜ Put the fruit in the basket. ｜ Walk slowly. ｜ Stop. ｜ Wait. ｜ Gentle hands. ｜ Safe body. ｜ Clean up. ｜ All done.\n\n## 3. Weekly Subthemes｜周次子主题\n\n### Week 1: Summer Care — Sun, Shade, Hat and Water｜第一周：夏季照护——太阳、阴凉、帽子和水\n\n儿童练习夏季身体照护常规：喝水、戴帽子、在阴凉处休息、擦、降温，以及通过真实常规回应\"hot／cool\"。\n\n### Week 2: Water Play — Wet, Dry, Pour, Scoop and Squeeze｜第二周：玩水——湿、干、倒、舀和挤\n\n儿童通过安全的感官游戏和杯子、海绵、毛巾、桶、托盘以及倒／舀常规来探索水。\n\n### Week 3: Picnic, Beach and Garden Materials｜第三周：野餐、海滩和花园材料\n\n儿童通过感官游戏、艺术、假装游戏和小世界建构来探索野餐篮、水果道具、餐巾、垫子、石头、叶子、贝壳、沙子道具、桶和花园材料。\n\n### Week 4: Outdoor Play, Helping and Safety｜第四周：户外游戏、帮忙与安全\n\n儿童整合夏季照护、玩水、野餐参与、花园／海滩材料、帮忙角色、收拾、户外动作、阴凉／休息常规和安全的热天游戏。\n\n## 4. Teacher Input Pool｜教师输入库\n\n### Summer Care and Weather Input｜夏季照护与天气输入\n\nsun ｜ shade ｜ hot ｜ cool ｜ hat ｜ water ｜ water bottle ｜ towel ｜ rest ｜ mat ｜ drink water ｜ stay in the shade ｜ put on your hat ｜ wipe ｜ dry ｜ cool down\n\n### Water, Picnic, Beach and Garden Input｜水、野餐、海滩和花园输入\n\nwater ｜ wet ｜ dry ｜ cup ｜ sponge ｜ bucket ｜ pour ｜ scoop ｜ squeeze ｜ fruit ｜ picnic ｜ basket ｜ napkin ｜ beach ｜ garden ｜ stone ｜ leaf ｜ shell ｜ sand ｜ plant\n\n### Action and Response Input｜动作与回应输入\n\nlook ｜ listen ｜ touch ｜ choose ｜ give ｜ put here ｜ put back ｜ drink water ｜ wipe ｜ pour ｜ scoop ｜ shake ｜ tap ｜ build ｜ walk slowly ｜ stop ｜ wait ｜ clean up ｜ gentle hands ｜ safe body ｜ helper\n\n### Teacher Routine Language｜教师常规语言\n\nDrink water. ｜ Hat on. ｜ Stay in the shade. ｜ It is hot. ｜ It is cool. ｜ Wet hands. ｜ Dry hands. ｜ Wipe with the towel. ｜ Pour water. ｜ Scoop water. ｜ Squeeze the sponge. ｜ Put it in the bucket. ｜ Picnic time. ｜ Walk slowly. ｜ Stop. ｜ Wait. ｜ Gentle hands. ｜ Safe body. ｜ Clean up. ｜ All done. ｜ Good job.\n\n## 5. Expected PK Response Modes｜预期 PK 反应方式\n\n儿童可能通过以下方式反应：看、指、在两个夏季道具间选择、命名或尝试熟悉的词、在帮助下戴帽子、喝水、擦或擦干手、在垫子上休息、倒、舀、挤海绵、把物品放进桶或篮子、分类湿／干材料、布置野餐垫、给杯子或水果道具、搭建海滩／花园场景、在户外慢走、按提示停下、等待轮流、帮忙收拾，以及在准备好时使用简短句块，例如 `drink water`、`wet hands`、`dry hands`、`in the bucket`、`safe body`、`all done`。\n\n不要求儿童解释夏季天气、描述海滩或花园概念、独立管理防晒安全、产出完整句子、比较天气条件、读标签，或完成基于练习册的夏季任务。\n\n## 6. Core Unit Songs｜单元核心歌曲\n\n### Core Songs｜核心歌曲\n\nRain Rain Go Away  \nI See Something Blue  \nWalking Walking  \nCount & Move  \nClean Up Song  \nPass the Beanbag  \nWalking In The Jungle  \nOpen Shut Them  \nThis Is The Way  \nThe Bath Song  \nAre You Hungry  \nThe More We Get Together\n\n### Use of Songs｜歌曲的使用\n\n歌曲应当支持玩水、慢走、停下、传递、野餐常规、擦、清洗、收拾、休息和共享帮忙。儿童在准备好时可以唱重复的词，但动作、手势、物品处理、倒、舀、摇、踏步、擦和收拾仍是有效的参与。\n\n## 7. Resource Opportunities｜资源机会\n\n### Useful EASTIE Spaces｜可用 EASTIE 空间\n\nClassroom  \nSmall outdoor grass area attached to PK classroom  \nOutdoor grass area  \nSandpit  \nIndoor physical fitness area  \nScience classroom / water tray area  \nArt classroom  \nPretend kitchen / home corner  \nConstruction / block room  \nMusic classroom  \nCircle-time area / mat area  \nTable activity area\n\n### Useful Materials｜可用材料\n\nHats  \nTowels  \nWater bottles  \nCups  \nSponges  \nWater basins  \nWater trays  \nBuckets  \nScoops  \nFunnels  \nNapkins  \nPicnic baskets  \nFruit props  \nPicnic mats  \nLeaves  \nStones  \nShells  \nSand props  \nGarden tools  \nToy plants  \nBlocks  \nLego / Duplo bricks  \nLarge wooden blocks  \nCloths  \nShakers  \nRhythm sticks  \nBlue scarves  \nVisual hot/cool cards  \nGo/stop cards  \nClean-up baskets\n\n### Enhanced Resource Opportunities｜拓展资源机会\n\n户外草地和附属的户外区域可以支持阴凉／休息常规、水壶常规、野餐游戏和慢动作游戏。科学教室或水盘区域可以支持倒、舀、挤和湿／干探索。沙坑和自然材料可以支持海滩和花园小世界游戏。艺术和建构空间可以支持夏季拼贴、海滩／花园搭建，以及如果需要的班级节日展示。\n\n## 8. Boundary Notes｜边界说明\n\n本单元不应变成天气科学单元。`sun`、`hot`、`cool`、`shade`、`wet`、`dry` 通过真实常规和材料来体验。\n\n本单元不应要求独立的口头输出。简短句块是基于准备度的。\n\n本单元不应使用不安全或无人监督的玩水。水的深度、滑倒、溅水、卫生、过敏和热暴露必须由成人控制。\n\n本单元不应过度包装野餐或海滩游戏。发展重点是具体参与、帮忙角色、感官探索、安全和收拾。\n\n## 9. Unit-Level Observation Focus｜单元观察重点\n\n教师观察儿童是否参与夏季照护常规、喝水、回应阴凉／休息提示、在支持下使用帽子和毛巾、安全地探索水、通过动作区分湿／干、受控地倒／舀／挤、处理野餐／海滩／花园材料、参与假装野餐或帮忙角色、搭建简单的夏季场景、在户外安全移动、等待轮流，以及加入收拾。\n\n观察应聚焦于身体照护意识、感官参与、自我调节、安全动作、生活技能、社交帮忙和准备度导向的交流，而非词汇掌握。"
};

